<LegalLayout
  lang="en"
  title="Proving your trail maintenance"
  description="How to keep a dated record of every reported problem and its fix, and produce it for your grant applications, your insurer or your board."
>
  <p>
    A grant application, an annual report, a question from an insurer: sooner or later, the crew is asked to
    show what it did on its trails. When the work lives in notebooks, emails and volunteers' memories,
    rebuilding it takes evenings.
  </p>

  <h2>Every problem keeps its history</h2>
  <p>
    In TrailSnag, every report keeps its state — to confirm, confirmed, fixed, rejected — and the history of
    every act taken on it. The territory stats show total, open, validated and fixed reports, and the average
    time to fix.
  </p>

  <h2>Your data, exportable on every tier</h2>
  <p>
    Export the log as CSV, GPX or KML on every tier, the free one included: it is your data, and you can take
    it with you whenever you want. The CSV opens in a spreadsheet; GPX and KML open in mapping tools.
  </p>

  <h2>The intervention report as a PDF</h2>
  <p>
    For a file, the intervention-report PDF lays out the work problem by problem. It is included from the Parc
    tier up. On the free tier, Halte, you produce one free per season (1 May to 31 October, then 1 November to
    30 April).
  </p>

  <h2>Showing the work to the public</h2>
  <p>
    When you choose to, your public map shows what is reported and what is resolved. Each trail's status —
    open, caution or closed — colours its line on the map, and a one-line embeddable map shows it on your own
    website.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <p>
    Claim your land: the history starts with the first report you receive, and everything after it is dated
    and exportable.
  </p>
  <p><a href={claimUrl}>Claim your land</a> · <a href="/en/pricing/">See pricing</a></p>
</LegalLayout>
